<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Einsiedeln Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Einsiedeln Abbey is a Benedictine monastery in the canton of Schwyz, founded in 934 on the site where the hermit Saint Meinrad had lived. Its shrine of the Black Madonna made it one of the chief pilgrimage centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A long dispute with Schwyz over pastures, the Marchenstreit, culminated in the Schwyzers' raid on the abbey in 1314, shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten">Battle of Morgarten</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/einsiedeln-abbey.jpg" alt="Einsiedeln Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Sattler martinsattler · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trachslau,_Schweiz_(Unsplash).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The history of the abbey begins with Meinrad of Einsiedeln, born in 797 at Sulchen and educated at the abbey school on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichenau-island">Reichenau Island</a>. He became a monk and priest, and after attracting attention for reported miracles he set up a hermitage in the Einsiedeln forest in 828, seeking privacy. He was murdered by two robbers in January 861. Other hermits occupied the site over the next eighty years. In 934 Eberhard, formerly Provost of Strassburg, built the abbey and church there and became its first abbot. According to legend, Christ himself consecrated the church in 948 in honour of his mother, and the pilgrimage began as one to the Chapel of the Saviour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 965 Emperor Otto I named the third abbot, Gregory, a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, a title his successors held until the empire's dissolution in 1806. After a fire the church was enlarged in 1226, with the lower church built over the Chapel of the Saviour. In the 13th century the pilgrimage became more popular and better organised, and an enthroned Madonna on an abbey seal of 1239 is regarded as the oldest miraculous image. The pilgrimage turned gradually into a Marian one, and its oldest surviving reference dates from the early 14th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> studied at the abbey, which remained unaffected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p>Discipline declined in the early 16th century until Abbot Ludovicus II, who held office from 1526 to 1544, restored stricter observance. Abbot Augustine I led the movement that created the Swiss Congregation of the Order of St. Benedict in 1602. Abbot Maurus rebuilt the church between 1704 and 1719, and the baroque ornamentation was finished in 1734. French revolutionary soldiers occupied the abbey in 1798, ending its status as an independent principality; the monks returned in 1801 and the abbey was reinstated on 19 February 1803 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a>. In the 1840s, fearing suppression, the monks sent a group to southern Indiana, which founded Saint Meinrad Archabbey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Einsiedeln is a Benedictine monastery and a territorial abbey, so it lies outside any diocese and its abbot governs with the authority of a diocesan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The Black Madonna in the Chapel of Grace draws pilgrims and visitors from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The library, founded in 934, holds around 230,000 printed books and 1,230 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and the abbey received its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in 1664. It also runs a school with a history of over a thousand years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> consecrated the new high altar in the lower choir on 15 June 1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Einsiedeln Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Schwyz quarrelled with <b>Einsiedeln Abbey</b> over pasture, expanded into the March and Höfe, and fought Zürich in the Old Zürich War.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Canton of Schwyz</a></li></ul></section>
